WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:27.071
<v Casey>Hey, welcome back. Today we've got three evacuation situations hitting the Cariboo Regional District in one week — Felker Lake, Quesnel, and now Clinton with Highway 97 closed — plus BC Hydro confirming wildfire ash is actually causing power flickers across the grid. Our Deep Dive: can one regional office really run three crises at once? That and more, coming right up.

00:00:52.371 --> 00:01:16.370
<v Casey>Welcome to Cariboo Signals, an AI-generated review of the latest tech news and ideas that impact our rural communities — I'm Casey. It's Saturday, July 25th, 2026. Coming to you from the Cariboo, on lands that have been cared for by the Secwépemc, Tŝilhqot'in, and Dakelh peoples since time immemorial. Today's theme is Cariboo Local Affairs.

00:01:17.670 --> 00:01:39.098
<v Riley>And I'm Riley. It's a heavy one this week — we're looking at how local governments in the Cariboo actually make decisions when several crises land on their desk at the same time, and whether the institutions we've built can keep up with the pace of a bad fire summer.

00:01:39.398 --> 00:02:18.398
<v Casey>Before we get into it, a quick weather check. Out at Horsefly Lake, our home base, it's fourteen degrees with clear skies this morning, heading to a high of twenty-five and a low of twelve tonight. Light drizzle expected tomorrow, about three millimetres of precipitation, so don't get too used to the blue sky. Across the region — 100 Mile House and Quesnel are both sitting at fifteen with clear skies, Williams Lake is at sixteen and clear, and out on the Chilcotin plateau, Hanceville's reporting fifteen degrees and mainly clear.

00:02:18.698 --> 00:02:33.697
<v Riley>Clear on paper, but with the fire activity we're about to cover, don't trust the blue sky too much today — a lot of that clarity depends on which way the wind's blowing the smoke.

00:02:33.997 --> 00:02:37.854
<v Casey>Let's get into what's actually happening on the ground.

00:02:39.354 --> 00:03:02.925
<v Riley>Fire and infrastructure are basically one story today, so let's start there and work outward. Enbridge broke ground this week on its four-billion-dollar Sunrise Expansion project near Prince George, with federal Energy Minister Tim Hodgson, Lheidli T'enneh Chief Dolleen Logan, Prince George Mayor Simon Yu, and Jobs Minister Ravi Kahlon all at the ceremony together.

00:03:03.225 --> 00:03:15.224
<v Casey>Four billion dollars is a big number for a groundbreaking photo. What's actually getting built, and when does anyone in the Cariboo see a job out of it?

00:03:15.524 --> 00:03:44.238
<v Riley>It's pipeline capacity expansion, and realistically it's years before it employs a single Cariboo welder directly. But it's a reminder that the major energy infrastructure decisions shaping this whole region get made in rooms most residents never see — and this time, at least, a First Nation chief was standing at the podium as a named partner in the announcement, not an afterthought mentioned in paragraph six.

00:03:44.538 --> 00:03:53.966
<v Casey>Whether that's meaningful partnership or a good photo op is a question worth asking again once the shovels are actually moving dirt.

00:03:55.466 --> 00:04:11.323
<v Riley>Over in the East Kootenay, the District of Invermere has opened the Columbia Valley Centre as a cooling and clean-air space, with both heat and smoke expected to linger in the forecast there for the foreseeable future.

00:04:11.623 --> 00:04:24.480
<v Casey>That's the kind of quiet civic decision that never makes a headline but matters enormously if you're an elderly resident without air conditioning and the air quality index is climbing.

00:04:24.780 --> 00:04:41.065
<v Riley>It's a small thing — opening a community hall with the air conditioning running — but it's exactly the kind of low-cost, high-impact local governance move that doesn't need a council vote or a press conference to matter.

00:04:42.565 --> 00:05:00.136
<v Casey>Closer to home, 100 Mile House had to cancel its 2026 Pride in the Park event because of wildfire smoke, according to the 100 Mile Free Press. The organizers appear to be running a scaled-down, first-come-first-served version of the gathering instead.

00:05:00.436 --> 00:05:14.150
<v Riley>That's a council-adjacent call too — when public health and air quality override a planned community event, somebody has to make that decision fast, without the luxury of a scheduled committee meeting.

00:05:14.450 --> 00:05:25.164
<v Casey>And it's a decision that disproportionately lands on volunteer organizers who don't have a communications department to manage the fallout, unlike a municipal government would.

00:05:26.664 --> 00:05:44.664
<v Riley>Down in Kamloops, the food bank's president, Bernadette Siracky, was on CFJC Midday this week talking about rising demand — she's underlining just how important both food and monetary donations are right now, especially with wildfire displacement adding pressure across the region.

00:05:44.964 --> 00:05:56.535
<v Casey>We'll come back to food security in a few minutes — today's Community Spotlight is Williams Lake's own Salvation Army, doing exactly that kind of work locally.

00:05:58.035 --> 00:06:30.606
<v Riley>There's a U.S. story worth flagging even though it's not something Canadians vote on directly — a new federal rule in the States ties potential green card approval to a person's SNAP benefit history, taking effect in mid-September. More than four million fewer Americans have been on food assistance since a major federal bill passed, a decline tied to both direct policy change and what advocates call a chilling effect on immigrant families applying at all.

00:06:30.906 --> 00:06:49.334
<v Casey>Worth watching for how policy design either amplifies or closes gaps for vulnerable people — not something we can act on locally, but the kind of precedent that has a way of echoing north eventually, especially in how provincial benefit programs get structured.

00:06:50.834 --> 00:07:19.976
<v Riley>And now the story that ties directly into today's Deep Dive — BC Hydro has confirmed that wildfire smoke and ash are physically causing flickering lights and power fluctuations across the province, including here in the Cariboo. Bob Gammer, BC Hydro's community relations coordinator, told CKPG News that part of the backbone of the grid runs right through some of the most fire-active parts of the Southern Interior.

00:07:20.276 --> 00:07:36.561
<v Casey>That's not a metaphor — ash landing on live insulator equipment can actually cause arcing and flashover on transmission lines. We'll dig into what that means for a region running on one grid backbone in a few minutes.

00:07:38.061 --> 00:07:59.918
<v Riley>Let's move to the closer-to-home story before we get there — Highway 97 is now closed at Clinton after new evacuation orders were issued for the Fiftynine Creek fire, part of the growing Big Bar wildfire complex north and west of town, as reported by the Williams Lake Tribune's Barbara Roden.

00:08:00.218 --> 00:08:14.789
<v Casey>That's the same complex that's been expanding all week. A highway closure at Clinton effectively cuts a shared north-south transportation corridor for a big chunk of the Cariboo, not just the immediate evacuation zone.

00:08:15.089 --> 00:08:36.089
<v Riley>And it sits right alongside the Felker Lake situation — My Cariboo Now is reporting the Cariboo Regional District has now put 330 parcels of land around Felker and Chimney Lakes under evacuation alert or order, with smoke from that fire visible from Williams Lake and Highway 97 itself.

00:08:36.389 --> 00:08:44.960
<v Casey>Three separate evacuation situations inside one regional district's jurisdiction, in the same week. That's the thread we're pulling on today.

00:08:46.460 --> 00:09:10.031
<v Riley>On a related note, the Williams Lake Tribune also reported that a wildland fire at the Williams Lake Stampede Grounds was extinguished Friday morning after being reported around 8:50 a.m. — a much smaller incident, but a reminder that fire risk this week isn't confined to the backcountry, it's showing up inside city limits too.

00:09:10.331 --> 00:09:15.045
<v Casey>Good outcome there, at least — caught early, put out fast.

00:09:16.545 --> 00:09:32.402
<v Riley>And in the Quesnel Cariboo Observer, parents are calling out the province for cutting funding to a 2SLGBTQ+ anti-bullying program in schools. They're framing it as the province quietly withdrawing support without much public explanation or consultation.

00:09:32.702 --> 00:09:47.701
<v Casey>That's a governance transparency question in its own right — a funding decision made in Victoria, felt in Quesnel classrooms, with parents left to piece together why through news coverage rather than a direct notice.

00:09:48.001 --> 00:10:03.858
<v Riley>Also worth noting — BC Gov News is urging residents province-wide to pre-register with Emergency Support Services given the elevated wildfire activity, which lines up directly with everything we've just covered about Felker Lake, Quesnel, and Clinton.

00:10:04.158 --> 00:10:14.443
<v Casey>That's a genuinely useful, low-effort thing residents can do this weekend — pre-registering takes minutes and means one less thing to figure out mid-evacuation.

00:10:15.943 --> 00:10:37.371
<v Riley>There's also a piece from Ricochet on how Ontario Premier Doug Ford's government left First Nations communities in northern Ontario without adequate evacuation warning during this summer's wildfires — a sobering contrast, frankly, to the coordination BC's regional districts are at least attempting this week, even with all their gaps.

00:10:37.671 --> 00:10:47.956
<v Casey>Worth sitting with — this isn't a uniquely BC problem, and it isn't automatically solved just because a warning system exists on paper somewhere.

00:10:48.256 --> 00:11:02.398
<v Riley>On a lighter note, New Atlas is reporting the world's new tallest timber tower — a hybrid "plyscraper" called Atlassian Central — just topped out in Australia at a hundred and eighty metres.

00:11:02.698 --> 00:11:05.269
<v Casey>A wooden skyscraper. Sure. Why not.

00:11:05.569 --> 00:11:19.711
<v Riley>And Engadget's covering a study showing EV batteries may be lasting up to forty percent longer than researchers originally predicted — turns out the industry had been running its degradation tests too pessimistically.

00:11:20.011 --> 00:11:25.582
<v Casey>Forty percent is a big miss for something people make purchasing decisions around.

00:11:25.882 --> 00:11:46.024
<v Riley>There's also continuing coverage of the AI open-weight policy fight in Washington — more than two dozen tech companies, including Nvidia and Mistral, sent a letter this week warning policymakers against broad restrictions on open-weight AI models as the U.S. debates its response to Chinese AI development.

00:11:46.324 --> 00:11:58.752
<v Casey>That's a fight with real implications for who gets to build and adapt these tools locally, but it's not a Cariboo governance story today, so we'll leave it there.

00:11:59.952 --> 00:12:27.809
<v Riley>Before we move to the spotlight — two emails landed in our inbox this week that turned out to be automated Google account verification notices, not real listener mail. If you're an actual human listening right now with a correction, a story tip, or a question about how your local government works, we genuinely want to hear it — feedback at cariboo signals dot c-a.

00:12:28.109 --> 00:12:36.251
<v Casey>Now, let's talk about an organization doing quiet, essential work in Williams Lake every single day of the year.

00:12:36.551 --> 00:13:21.122
<v Riley>The Salvation Army in Williams Lake runs the local food bank, an emergency shelter, and free community meals year-round — not just the Christmas hamper program most people associate them with. With wildfire evacuations displacing families across the region right now, and the Kamloops Food Bank already reporting rising demand this week, organizations like this one are the actual front line when a crisis hits and nobody's quite sure who to call. They rely on donations of food, money, and volunteer time to keep the doors open, and they need that support most in exactly the weeks when the headlines are all about fire.

00:13:21.422 --> 00:13:36.421
<v Casey>When evacuation orders hit, it's places like the Salvation Army that quietly absorb the overflow nobody budgets for in advance — worth remembering that the fire coverage tonight has a human face behind it locally.

00:13:36.721 --> 00:13:41.863
<v Riley>Which is actually a good bridge into what we're digging into today.

00:13:42.163 --> 00:14:18.163
<v Riley>Here's the actual question worth sitting with this week: when the Cariboo Regional District has to issue evacuation orders or alerts in three different places inside seven days — Felker Lake, an area northwest of Quesnel, and now Clinton with Highway 97 closed — does the region actually have enough institutional capacity to make those calls well? Or is one regional district office being asked to run three simultaneous emergencies with staffing built for one bad week a season, not three in a row?

00:14:18.463 --> 00:14:37.320
<v Casey>That's the real question. Not "did they warn people in time" — mostly, they did. It's whether the same handful of people signing an evacuation order for Felker Lake on Tuesday can give Clinton residents the same quality of attention and follow-up by Friday.

00:14:37.620 --> 00:14:57.762
<v Riley>BC Wildfire Service's own Spring 2026 Seasonal Outlook flagged the western Cariboo months ago as primed for very large fires, specifically because of elevated multi-year drought conditions. So the warning system worked in the sense that this wasn't a surprise to anyone paying attention to the modeling.

00:14:58.062 --> 00:15:14.347
<v Casey>The outlook predicted it. The institution still had to scramble in real time. Knowing a flood is coming months out and actually having the sandbags stacked and the staff rota built for it are two very different things.

00:15:15.147 --> 00:15:39.146
<v Riley>Fair distinction, but look at how the Felker Lake situation actually unfolded — 330 parcels around Felker and Chimney Lakes moved from alert to order in a documented, staged progression. That's the alert system doing exactly what it's designed to do: giving people days of lead time instead of a siren at three in the morning.

00:15:39.446 --> 00:16:06.017
<v Casey>Except the CRD board and its emergency operations staff are the same people simultaneously covering the Quesnel-area evacuation order, and now presumably fielding calls about the Clinton situation too — even though Clinton technically sits closer to the Thompson-Nicola Regional District's side of the Big Bar wildfire complex. Who's actually coordinating across that jurisdictional boundary in real time, this week, right now?

00:16:06.317 --> 00:16:37.174
<v Riley>That's a genuinely open question, and it's not academic. Highway 97 closing at Clinton cuts a transportation corridor both regional districts depend on for everything from grocery deliveries to medical transport. If TNRD is managing the Clinton evacuation order and CRD is managing Felker Lake and Quesnel simultaneously, somebody needs a shared operational picture across that line, or you risk two separate press releases contradicting each other about the same closed highway.

00:16:37.474 --> 00:16:38.474
<v Casey>Right.

00:16:38.774 --> 00:17:05.345
<v Riley>The province's Ministry of Emergency Management and Climate Readiness does maintain interagency coordination protocols specifically for multi-jurisdiction wildfire complexes like this one. Whether those protocols are actually being exercised at the working level this week, rather than just existing as a document somewhere, is the harder question — and it's one we genuinely don't have the answer to from this week's coverage.

00:17:05.645 --> 00:17:28.787
<v Casey>And that's before you even get to BC Hydro's side of it. Their community relations coordinator basically confirmed on the record that the grid's physical backbone runs through active fire zones — ash causing arcing on insulators isn't hypothetical anymore, it's happening this week, to real customers losing power or watching their lights flicker.

00:17:29.587 --> 00:17:52.301
<v Riley>Which is actually the more encouraging half of that story, in a strange way. BC Hydro proactively explained the cause to CKPG News instead of leaving residents to assume their neighborhood transformer was simply failing or their wiring was bad. In a week full of confusing, overlapping alerts, that's a genuine transparency win.

00:17:52.601 --> 00:18:18.743
<v Casey>It's a transparency win about a vulnerability they can't actually fix quickly. Knowing why your lights are flickering doesn't give you grid redundancy. A region this size effectively runs whole communities off one transmission backbone — if that backbone takes real physical damage during a bad fire week, not just flicker-level interference, what's the actual contingency plan, and who's published it?

00:18:19.043 --> 00:18:32.757
<v Riley>BC Hydro does maintain mobile generation units and emergency response crews that get pre-positioned during active wildfire seasons — that's standard utility practice for a complex of this size. It's not nothing.

00:18:33.057 --> 00:18:48.485
<v Casey>Pre-positioned generation covering how many hours of outage, for how many communities simultaneously? Nobody's published that number this week, and I'd want to see it in writing before calling this problem handled rather than just acknowledged.

00:18:49.285 --> 00:19:13.284
<v Riley>Honestly, I can't answer that — it isn't in anything published this week, and I'm not going to invent a number just to make the point land cleaner than it actually is. That's a legitimate records request somebody in the region should be making right now, not a guess either of us should offer on air.

00:19:14.484 --> 00:19:44.483
<v Casey>And that's actually the difference between this week and where we left this conversation a couple of weeks back. That earlier discussion was about legibility — whether ordinary residents could trace a decision after the fact, find the minutes, understand who approved what. This week isn't really about the paper trail at all. It's about whether the institution has enough hands on deck during a live, compounding crisis, full stop.

00:19:44.783 --> 00:20:04.497
<v Riley>Which is a fair pivot from where that conversation ended. That one was about traceability. This one's about raw capacity — literally, how many trained emergency operations staff does the CRD have on call when three separate fire situations demand attention in the same seven-day window?

00:20:04.797 --> 00:20:27.082
<v Casey>And I'd guess the honest answer is: fewer than three fires' worth, by a wide margin. Regional districts this size typically run emergency operations with a small core team that gets massively supplemented by provincial wildfire crews, mutual aid from neighboring districts, and volunteer fire departments the moment things spike like this.

00:20:27.382 --> 00:20:59.524
<v Riley>Which actually argues for something concrete and specific rather than another general call for "more resourcing." Mutual aid agreements between CRD and TNRD, specifically covering shared highway corridors like the Clinton stretch of Highway 97, negotiated in the off-season rather than improvised mid-fire. That's not a huge budget ask — it's a governance design question about who has the authority to issue a single joint notice when one highway serves residents of two separate jurisdictions.

00:20:59.824 --> 00:21:27.681
<v Casey>That I'd actually back without much hesitation. It's not glamorous, it won't get a press conference of its own, but a pre-negotiated joint-notice protocol between adjacent regional districts is exactly the kind of unglamorous fix that would matter enormously the next time this happens — and given the drought pattern BC Wildfire Service is already tracking, it will happen again, probably before the decade's out.

00:21:28.481 --> 00:21:40.480
<v Riley>The Wildfire Service's own seasonal outlook makes that close to certain — this isn't a one-summer anomaly. It's the emerging shape of Cariboo summers under sustained multi-year drought.

00:21:40.780 --> 00:22:09.494
<v Casey>Which is exactly why the fix has to be structural rather than reactive. You can't staff up emergency operations centres for the worst week of a bad decade and leave that staffing in place year-round — nobody's going to fund that. But you can build the cross-jurisdictional agreement once, in January, when nobody's evacuating anybody, and have it ready to activate the next time three fires overlap.

00:22:09.794 --> 00:22:48.365
<v Riley>And that's actually a question residents can push on directly, not something that requires waiting for Victoria to act. The next Cariboo Regional District board meeting is open to the public. Anyone in Clinton, Williams Lake, Horsefly, or Quesnel who wants to know whether that cross-jurisdictional coordination protocol with TNRD actually exists can ask the question on the record, and the answer becomes part of the public minutes — which, incidentally, brings us right back to that legibility question from a couple of weeks ago, just from a different angle.

00:22:48.665 --> 00:23:07.950
<v Casey>If it doesn't exist yet, that's a small, cheap, entirely achievable thing to build before next fire season — not a grant application, not new hires, just a signed agreement between two regional districts that already talk to each other constantly anyway on other files.

00:23:08.250 --> 00:23:21.107
<v Riley>Small, unglamorous, and exactly the kind of thing that gets zero attention until the highway's already closed and somebody's stuck between two press releases that don't agree with each other.

00:23:21.407 --> 00:23:41.978
<v Casey>Which might be the actual test of whether Cariboo local governance is keeping pace with the climate it's now operating in — not whether the warnings go out on time, but whether the institutions behind those warnings can talk to each other before the fire forces them to.

00:23:43.178 --> 00:24:00.320
<v Riley>On that note — if you've got a correction, a story tip, or you want to weigh in on how your own community's emergency coordination actually works on the ground, write to us at feedback at cariboo signals dot c-a.

00:24:00.620 --> 00:24:12.191
<v Casey>Stay safe out there with the smoke and the fire activity this week, and keep an eye on those evacuation alerts. Hope you're having a great weekend.